{
  "identity": {
    "name": "Thérèse of Lisieux",
    "slug": "therese-of-lisieux-lean",
    "religious_name": "Sister Thérèse of the Child Jesus and of the Holy Face",
    "tier": "A",
    "tradition": "Catholic"
  },
  "saint": {
    "name": "St. Thérèse of Lisieux",
    "slug": "therese-of-lisieux-lean",
    "summary": "On the evening of 30 September 1897, a prioress sent a dying nun's community out of the infirmary, thinking the end was far off, then had the bell rung to call them back. Thérèse Martin was twenty-four. She had promised to spend her Heaven doing good on earth and to let fall a shower of roses. Who was this young Carmelite, who stamped her foot at a maid, wept at a Christmas remark, and broke the rule of silence to ask the Pope for her convent? Follow her from a night of tears to a fog of doubt, and find out what became of her promise.",
    "biography": "<p>On the evening of 30 September 1897, in the infirmary of the Carmelite convent at Lisieux in Normandy, the prioress looked at the young nun in the bed and judged that the end was still some hours away. The community had been gathered there since about five, when the agony began. Céline, the dying woman’s closest sister, had wet her lips with a piece of ice, and the patient had looked up at her. Now Mother Marie de Gonzague sent the sisters out, and they filed from the room.</p>\n<p>The young nun asked whether this was not the agony, and a few minutes later the bell rang again and the doors were opened. The sisters came back and stood around the bed, and at about twenty past seven Thérèse Martin, twenty-four years old, died of tuberculosis.</p>\n<p>In the weeks before, she had made a promise that her sisters wrote down. She would spend her Heaven doing good on earth, she said, and she would let fall a shower of roses. It was a large thing for a nun to say. She had lived behind convent walls since the age of fifteen, her only writings were still in a drawer, and hardly anyone outside Lisieux had heard her name. What could come of a promise like that? The answer took twenty-eight years to arrive, and when it came it came in St. Peter’s Basilica, in front of a crowd that filled the square outside.</p>\n<p>The Catholic Church canonized her in 1925 and named her a Doctor of the Church in 1997. She is co-patron of the missions, although she never left her convent, and co-patron of France. Her autobiography has been translated into about fifty languages, and yet the life behind it was short, hidden, and mostly ordinary.</p>\n<p>What set her apart was a simple claim with a hard edge. A person does not have to climb to God by great deeds, she said; God lifts the small, and the small should stay small. She tested that idea in a laundry and in a refectory. She tested it again through a long dark stretch at the end of her life, when she could feel no certainty that Heaven existed, and she wrote down the doubt as plainly as the joy. This is the story of how a sensitive, stubborn girl became that writer, and of what happened to her promise afterward.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"youngest-of-nine\">The Youngest of Nine</h2>\n<p>Marie Françoise Thérèse Martin was born on 2 January 1873 in Alençon and baptized two days later. Louis and Zélie Martin had nine children, and she was the youngest. Five daughters grew up, and every one of them would become a nun. Her parents, as it happens, are saints of the Church themselves, canonized together on 18 October 2015.</p>\n<p>At home she was called Thérèse, long before a book made the world call her the Little Flower, and she was a child with a will of her own. A list of her faults that Monsignor de Teil drew up in 1910 for her cause names stubbornness and pride at the age of three. It is a little unusual for the papers that support a saint to admit that much so early, and they do not soften it.</p>\n<p>She told one story against herself. The maid Victoire, asked to lead the prayers at the little May altar, did nothing but look at her and laugh, and Thérèse stamped her foot at her. Then came the tears, first of anger and then of honest regret, because the small Thérèse felt everything at full strength and could hide none of it.</p>\n<p>In 1877 her mother died, and the family left Alençon for Lisieux to be near her uncle Guérin, settling in a house called Les Buissonnets. Thérèse was four and a half. She wrote later that around that age she lost the strength of mind she had had before, and became a child who cried easily.</p>\n<p>Her sister Pauline took her mother’s place. Thérèse called her, in her own account, her second mother, and Pauline taught her, looked after her, and listened to her. At eight and a half she began school at the Benedictine Abbey in Lisieux, taking the place of her sister Léonie, who had been leaving.</p>\n<p>Then one day Pauline told her that she meant to enter the Carmelite convent in Lisieux. The Carmelites are an order of nuns who live enclosed, in silence and prayer, and once a sister went through that door her family saw her only through a grille. Thérèse understood at once what it meant for her. She was about to lose a second mother, and within months she was ill in a way that nobody in the family knew how to name.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"a-smile-in-the-sickroom\">A Smile in the Sickroom</h2>\n<p>In 1883, when she was ten, Thérèse lay sick for weeks, and the family feared for her life. Her father paid for a novena of Masses at Notre-Dame des Victoires in Paris, a church known for prayer to the Virgin Mary. At home her sisters knelt by the bed and prayed to the Virgin themselves. Thérèse called for her sister Marie again and again.</p>\n<p>Marie came in, and Thérèse saw her enter but could not recognize her. Marie knelt down with the others. It was then, Thérèse wrote, that the Virgin appeared to her, more beautiful than anything she had ever seen, and smiled at her. She looked down from that sight and found Marie watching her with love. Marie thought her cured, and Thérèse remembered that the healing came gently, without any violence, and not all at once.</p>\n<p>She called it a miracle of Our Lady of Victories, since her father’s novena had been offered there. When Pope John Paul II wrote about her in 1997 he listed the event among the graces of her life. The Church has never counted it among the miracles of her cause, and it rests on her own report and on the word of the family who were in the room.</p>\n<p>The sources disagree about the day. In her own list of the graces of her life she gives 10 May 1883, while the date usually repeated is 13 May, which was Pentecost that year. The difference is a matter of days, and the story is the same whichever one you choose.</p>\n<p>The cure did not make her any less sensitive. She made her First Communion on 8 May 1884 and was confirmed on 14 June of the same year, and in those months she kept her wish for Carmel. She kept her tears as well, and the tears were about to meet the most important Christmas of her life.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"the-shoes-by-the-chimney\">The Shoes by the Chimney</h2>\n<p>In the Martin house, Christmas still ended the way it had when Thérèse was small. After midnight Mass the family came home, and the youngest daughter went to the chimney, where her shoes waited with presents inside, and the whole family enjoyed watching her open them. In 1886 that youngest daughter was nearly fourteen.</p>\n<p>Her father was tired. As she climbed the stairs to take off her hat she heard him below, looking at the shoes: “Enfin, heureusement que c’est la dernière année!” Well, luckily this is the last year.</p>\n<p>It was a small, weary remark, the kind any father makes. For Thérèse it should have been a disaster. For years any cross word had reduced her to tears, and the tears to more tears, until someone had to comfort her out of them. Céline, following her up the stairs, saw her eyes fill and knew what would happen next. “Oh! Thérèse, don’t come down,” she said. “It would hurt you too much to look in your shoes right now.”</p>\n<p>Thérèse went down anyway. She went to the chimney and joyfully pulled out every present, looking, in her own words, as happy as a queen. Her father laughed with her. Nobody in the room knew what had just happened, but she did. The work she had failed to do in ten years, she wrote, Jesus had done in one instant.</p>\n<p>She called it her Christmas conversion. Nothing about it made her hard, and the change turned her eyes outward, away from her own hurt and toward other people. The next year she found someone to turn them toward, and she found him in a newspaper.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"her-first-sinner\">Her First Sinner</h2>\n<p>In the summer of 1887 a murderer named Pranzini was condemned to death, and Thérèse, who was fourteen, decided to pray for him. She called him, in her own account, her first child. A girl in Lisieux could not know whether she had changed anything for him, so Thérèse asked for one sign: some mark of repentance at the very end, to show that her prayer had been heard.</p>\n<p>The day after the execution she opened the newspaper La Croix. On the scaffold, it said, he had taken the crucifix that the priest held out to him and kissed it three times. Thérèse read the lines and had to hide her tears.</p>\n<p>She did not claim to have converted him, and she wrote only that she had asked for a sign and that her prayer was answered to the letter. The Church has never counted this among miracles. It is her private story, but it shaped the way she understood prayer for the rest of her life, as something small and hidden that could reach far.</p>\n<p>She had also begun to want something that everyone told her was too early. She was not yet fifteen, and she wanted to enter Carmel. Exactly one person in the world would take her side, and that person was her father.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"kneeling-before-the-pope\">Kneeling Before the Pope</h2>\n<p>The first step was the bishop. On 31 October 1887 Thérèse and her father traveled to Bayeux to see Bishop Hugonin, who received them very coldly and asked her to wait. Her father took her part in the room, and the two of them left with nothing settled. Thérèse had no intention of waiting.</p>\n<p>In November she went to Rome with a pilgrimage, her father at her side, and she carried a plan in her head. She would ask the Pope himself. The rules of the audience forbade pilgrims to speak to him, and she had decided to break them.</p>\n<p>The audience took place on 20 November 1887. When her turn came, Thérèse knelt before Leo XIII and rested her hands on his knee. She asked him to let her enter Carmel, and she told him that everyone would agree if only he would say yes. “Well, well! You will enter if it is God’s will,” he answered, in the words she recorded, and it was not the answer she had come for.</p>\n<p>Before she could say more, a Noble Guard lifted her away. The Pope laid his hand on her lips as she went. Outside she wept, and she wrote afterward that in the depths of her heart she felt peace while bitterness filled her soul.</p>\n<p>In her account of that trip she remembered a prayer she had made to the Child Jesus for some time before, asking him to use her as a plaything.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>“Like a little ball of no value, that could be thrown on the ground, kicked about, pierced, left in a corner.”</p>\n<p><cite>— Thérèse of Lisieux, Story of a Soul, Manuscript A (1912 English translation), on her prayer to the Child Jesus</cite></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>She meant it as a picture of surrender, and the picture would return. On the road home from Rome she had every reason to feel that she had been left in a corner herself. The Pope had said neither no nor yes, and she went back to Lisieux with half an answer.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"doors-closing-at-carmel\">Doors Closing at Carmel</h2>\n<p>The rest of the answer came in the spring. On 9 April 1888, the Feast of the Annunciation, moved from its usual day, Thérèse entered the Carmel of Lisieux. She was fifteen years and three months old.</p>\n<p>That morning her father blessed her as she knelt in front of him, and he was weeping, and so was she. He had stood beside her at Bayeux and in Rome, and now he gave her up. She walked through the door of the enclosure and it closed behind her, and on the other side she received the embraces of the sisters who had been mothers to her.</p>\n<p>She had asked for this life at an age when most girls were still at school. Clothed in the habit on 10 January 1889, she took her vows on 8 September 1890, and on the day of her profession she carried a letter on her heart. In it she asked Jesus to take her from this world rather than let her stain the robe she had received at baptism. The letter shows what she feared most, which was to lose the clean state of a child.</p>\n<p>At home her father was failing. He fell ill and was paralysed, and he died on 29 July 1894. His last word to her, in her record, was “In Heaven!”</p>\n<p>Inside the walls she had to live, day after day, with women she had not chosen, some of them old and some of them ill. Her ideas would not be tested by a single act of courage. They would be tested in an evening walk to the refectory and in an afternoon in the laundry.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"laundry-water-and-sister-peter\">Laundry Water and Sister Peter</h2>\n<p>In the novitiate Thérèse set herself an odd training. She resolved to do each small task for the sake of Jesus, to let no one see the effort, and to be kind to the sisters she liked least. She describes the experiment in her autobiography with a dry humor that most readers miss.</p>\n<p>There was an old nun, Sister St. Peter, who was ill and hard to please and could walk only with help. Each evening, ten minutes before six, someone had to lead her to the refectory, and Thérèse offered. She took the old sister slowly to her place, and kept at it although Sister St. Peter was not an easy companion, and smiled at her every time. In the end the old nun was won over, and one evening she smiled back.</p>\n<p>The laundry set another trap. One day the sister working opposite her splashed her face with dirty water, and Thérèse first drew back and wiped it. Then it struck her that she was foolish to refuse such a gift, and she wrote that she tried hard to want more of it.</p>\n<p>She was not always so calm. She could be sharp with the younger sisters she was asked to help. Once, seeing a novice walk slowly toward the laundry, she asked her, more or less, whether that was how people hurry when they have children. When a sister complained about the noise she made, she wanted badly to defend herself, and then she thought that if she began to justify herself she would certainly lose her peace of mind. She also felt that she lacked the virtue to be accused in silence. Her last chance of safety, she said, was flight, so she left the room and sat down nearby with her heart pounding.</p>\n<p>She taught the same lesson with a story from her childhood. A horse stood at the garden gate, her friends argued about how to get past it, and Thérèse slipped between its legs while they talked. Years later she told the story to a novice who was tempted and struggling. Do not try to climb over the difficulty, she said, but pass beneath it, and we shall dry ourselves in the sunshine of love. The advice is typical of her. She did not ask a struggling person to become strong. She asked the person to go low.</p>\n<p>Prayer, too, went badly for her at times. Often she fell asleep during the hours set aside for it, and it did not frighten her, because a child, she wrote, is dear to its father whether it is asleep or awake. Of the dry feelings that came in prayer she said that she was no saint.</p>\n<p>From these years comes one of her best-known sayings about small things. She said it to a novice, who wrote it down.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>“If you feel too lazy to pick up a bit of thread, and yet do so for love of Jesus, you acquire more merit than for a much nobler action done in a moment of fervour.”</p>\n<p><cite>— Thérèse of Lisieux, in Counsels and Reminiscences, recorded by a novice</cite></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It is easy to see what she meant. Nobody important looks twice at a thread on a convent floor, and she was telling the sisters that such things were the work. Before long she would be asked to set that idea down on paper for readers she would never meet.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"a-little-way-to-heaven\">A Little Way to Heaven</h2>\n<p>Her sister Pauline, who was now Mother Agnes, asked her to write down her childhood memories, and Thérèse agreed. She wrote what became the first of three manuscripts, meant at the time for her sisters alone. Later the three would be joined, edited, and published as a single book.</p>\n<p>On 9 June 1895 she also made a private act of offering herself to Merciful Love, which she called her Act of Oblation, and her sisters kept it among her prayers. She was working toward an idea that she could not yet state plainly. It began with a fact about herself, which was that she was too small to climb to Heaven by great effort, and she could see no reason to pretend otherwise.</p>\n<p>Her confidence in God had a stubborn side. She told a novice that one can never have too much of it, because God is so mighty and so merciful. She had favorite saints, and they were not the ones a reader would expect. Her special patrons in Heaven, she said, were those who, so to speak, stole it, and she named the Holy Innocents and the Good Thief. A saint who thinks of Heaven as something to be taken by trust will not be surprised to say later, as she did, that God had earned her own crown for her.</p>\n<p>Her readers would call this her little way, although she announced no new teaching and said only that she had found something she could do. She wrote of it with playfulness. The same pages speak of a ball, a top, and ninepins, as if she were playing a game with God and meant to keep on playing.</p>\n<p>Her way had not yet been tried by anything heavier than a splash of laundry water. That test began in the spring of 1896, after midnight on Good Friday.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"a-sign-in-the-night\">A Sign in the Night</h2>\n<p>After midnight on Good Friday in 1896, Thérèse came back to her cell from the vigil at the altar of repose. As she lay down she felt something rise to her lips, and she thought it was blood. She was twenty-three. The lamp was out, so she waited until morning to be sure, and at the window she saw that she had been right.</p>\n<p>Her reaction is one of the more surprising scenes in her writing, because she was glad. Her soul was flooded with joy, she wrote, since she took it as a first call from Jesus on the anniversary of his death. The next night brought the same sign. At the chapter of pardons that morning she told the prioress of her hope of Heaven, and she added that she was not suffering.</p>\n<p>In the joyful days of the Easter season that followed, something else changed. The consolation she had felt in prayer left her, and she could describe what remained only as a fog. She asked her reader to suppose a person born in a land of thick fogs. She wrote that she sat at the table of bitterness where poor sinners take their food.</p>\n<p>This was the night of faith that she lived in for about a year and a half, until she died. She did not lose her trust, only the feeling of it, and she went on praying and working as though she believed, without comfort. Her sisters heard her say that she had no consolation and that her joys lay only in the depths of her soul. She asked that this be told after her death, so that other souls would not be discouraged.</p>\n<p>The name Little Flower suggests sweetness, and her own words show a woman who spent long stretches in the dark. She kept up her cheerfulness by choice. She said once that she acted cheerful when she could, because it gave pleasure to others.</p>\n<p>It was in those months, in September 1896, that she wrote a letter to her sister Marie, called Sister Marie of the Sacred Heart, which became her second manuscript. She was searching it for her place in the Church. The answer came to her in a single line.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>“O Jesus, my Love, at last I have found my vocation. My vocation is love!”</p>\n<p><cite>— Thérèse of Lisieux, Story of a Soul, Manuscript B (1912 English translation)</cite></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A few lines later the sentence comes in French: “Dans le cœur de l’Église, ma Mère, je serai l’Amour.” In English, in the heart of the Church, my Mother, I will be Love. She had found it in the fog, without any feeling to support it.</p>\n<p>Her playfulness survived the dark. At Christmas 1896 she left a top and a letter for a novice, addressed to her beloved little spouse, player of ninepins on the Mountain of Carmel.</p>\n<p>In June 1897 the prioress, Mother Marie de Gonzague, asked her to write about her life in religion. Thérèse began on the second or third of June, and by the fourth she had put everything else aside. She wrote in a black notebook, and she knew that these pages would be used when the community came to announce her death. In them she found her best picture of what she had been trying to say about smallness.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>“We live in an age of inventions; nowadays the rich need not trouble to climb the stairs, they have lifts instead.”</p>\n<p><cite>— Thérèse of Lisieux, Story of a Soul, Manuscript C (1912 English translation)</cite></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>She, too, wanted a lift to raise her to Jesus. She found it in a line of Scripture about a mother who carries her child. In her French the sentence reads that his arms, O Jesus, are the lift that must raise her to Heaven. For that, she added, she did not need to grow. On the contrary, she had to remain little and become smaller still. A modern reader may smile at the picture, and she meant it to be that simple. She did not finish the notebook.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"a-promise-of-roses\">A Promise of Roses</h2>\n<p>The last months are the best recorded of her life. From May to September 1897 Mother Agnes took down what Thérèse said, in pencil, on loose sheets, and the notes were later published in English as Novissima Verba. They cannot be read as neutral, because Mother Agnes was a sister, an editor, and a woman who wanted her sister to be a saint. Even so, they give us dates and scenes that nobody else recorded.</p>\n<p>On 27 May Thérèse said that she welcomed the circular letter that the convent would send out on her death, and that she could not understand sisters who refused one. On 30 May, with the prioress’s leave, she told Mother Agnes the secret of that Good Friday night, and Mother Agnes understood for the first time that the disease was an old one. On 5 June she said that if they found her dead one morning they should not worry, because the good God had come to fetch her. On 9 June she told her sister Marie that she would let fall a shower of roses after her death.</p>\n<p>At the start of July the bleeding returned, two or three times a day for a month, and on 8 July she was moved to the infirmary. The next day Canon Maupas, the priest in charge, came to consider giving her the last sacrament. She was so cheerful that he put it off. She told him, smiling, that she had not earned her crown, because God had earned it for her, and she said she did not know the trade of winning one’s point. The sacrament followed the day after.</p>\n<p>The doctor who looked after her, Dr. de Cornière, said that her suffering was dreadful, and he was amazed at her patience and her smile. Thérèse would not accept the praise. She denied that she was patient, and later she told a sister that she was no angel, because angels cannot suffer. She said also that she suffered only from moment to moment, and that discouragement comes from looking at the past or the future.</p>\n<p>On 15 July a dead ember from a lamp was used to light the candle of her cousin Marie Guérin, and Thérèse saw a picture in it. One small light can light all the candles, she said, and that is how hidden souls stand behind the saints and the Doctors of the Church. Two days later, on 17 July, Mother Agnes recorded the sentence that became her best-known promise.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>“Je veux passer mon Ciel à faire du bien sur la terre.”</p>\n<p><cite>— Thérèse of Lisieux, 17 July 1897, as recorded by Mother Agnes (French from the 1911 edition; in English: I want to spend my Heaven doing good on earth)</cite></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>She knew how it sounded. When a sister said that she would look down on them from Heaven, Thérèse answered that she would come down. To a novice she said that if her little way led anyone wrong, she would return to earth and tell them to take another road.</p>\n<p>She kept her jokes to the end. She told her sisters that she had said certain things only to frighten them, and she corrected a novice for sitting sideways on a chair. For the novices she had been something like a teacher, and she stayed one while she was dying. She also named her own days of trial.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>“Do you know which are my Sundays and feast-days? They are the days on which God tries me most.”</p>\n<p><cite>— Thérèse of Lisieux, in Counsels and Reminiscences, recorded by a novice</cite></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The other sayings of those weeks show a woman who would not play the part others wanted for her. She said she would not wish to die right after Communion, because little souls could not copy that. She looked at the crucifix and said she preferred to see Christ dead, because then he suffers no more. A sister was told not to lament by her bed, and when the others spoke of the Czar’s reception in France, Thérèse wanted to talk of God. She asked not to cough so that Céline could sleep, and she told the Virgin that she would love her even if refused.</p>\n<p>On 19 August she received her last Communion. Mother Agnes’s pencil notes give that date, and she says the printed autobiography is wrong to give 16 August. In those weeks Thérèse said she feared for her mind. She did not want to be praised for her virtues, because she was afraid of a proud thought, of saying to herself that she had acquired a virtue.</p>\n<p>On 6 September she was given a relic of Blessed Théophane Vénard, and she told his story. On 8 September, the anniversary of her profession, a robin flew into the infirmary and sat on her bed, and a sister brought wildflowers. On 14 September she stripped the petals from a rose over her crucifix and asked the sisters to gather them carefully, because they would give pleasure later. Those petals would have a place in the story after her death, and there were only two weeks left.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"the-last-evening\">The Last Evening</h2>\n<p>On the eve of her death, 29 September, Thérèse asked the prioress whether this was the agony and how she should set about dying. Later, when Mother Agnes read the prayers for the dying and reached the mention of devils, she made a mock threat with her hand. To Céline, who asked for a word of farewell, she whispered that all was said and finished, and that love alone counted.</p>\n<p>The next day, at about three in the afternoon, she lay with her arms out in the shape of a cross. The prioress laid a picture of Our Lady of Mount Carmel on her knees. That afternoon, in Mother Agnes’s record, she said that she had never sought anything but the truth and that she did not repent of giving herself up to Love. At about five the agony began and the community crowded into the infirmary. Céline put a piece of ice to her lips, and Thérèse looked at her.</p>\n<p>Then came the moment with which this story began. Shortly after seven the prioress sent the sisters away, thinking the end far off, and Thérèse asked her whether this was not the agony. The prioress said that it was, but that it might last some hours. “I do not wish to suffer less,” Thérèse answered, in Mother Agnes’s record. She looked at her crucifix, and the words that the 1912 English edition gives as her last were these.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>“Oh! I love Him! My God, I love Thee!”</p>\n<p><cite>— Thérèse of Lisieux, last words, 30 September 1897, as recorded by Mother Agnes</cite></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>She fell back with her head a little to one side. The sisters thought it was over, and the prioress had the infirmary bell rung to bring the community back, and the doors were opened. The sisters came in and knelt around the bed. In Mother Agnes’s account they saw a last ecstasy that lasted for the length of a Credo, her face like a lily, and at about twenty past seven she breathed her last.</p>\n<p>Those who were in the room remembered two more things, and both have been repeated ever since. Mother Agnes says that birds sang in the tree outside through the whole of the agony, and that the sky cleared at dusk, and the sisters confirm the birds. The Church makes no miracle claim about it. It is what the people in the room remembered.</p>\n<p>When the sisters washed the body, they thought she looked twelve or thirteen years old. On 4 October she was buried in the municipal cemetery of Lisieux, in the new Carmelite plot, and Taylor says she was the first Carmelite buried there. A palm branch was put in her hand, and Mother Agnes noted that the limbs stayed supple until the burial. The community now had a duty to perform, and the duty had a history of its own.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"a-book-goes-out\">A Book Goes Out</h2>\n<p>When a Carmel lost a sister, it sent a circular letter to other convents, a short life of the dead nun. The plan for Thérèse’s circular had begun in May, at the moment she told Mother Agnes the secret of her first hemorrhage, and it grew into a book. The pages that she had written for her sisters alone were about to go to a wider public.</p>\n<p>In 1898 the book appeared as <em>Histoire d’une âme</em>, in English <em>Story of a Soul</em>. Made from the three manuscripts, the childhood memories, the letter to Marie, and the unfinished black notebook, it was edited by Mother Agnes and others. The Carmel of Lisieux now shows the 1898 pages beside the original, and the comparison reveals additions and removed sentences.</p>\n<p>It sold quickly. By 1926 the Carmel counted more than a million French copies. An English version by Dziewicki came out in 1901, and a shorter English text by Taylor, first published in 1911, had reached 175,000 copies by 1926. The first English edition of <em>Soeur Thérèse</em> appeared at Christmas 1912 in 9,000 copies. In 1997 Pope John Paul II noted that the book had been translated into about fifty languages.</p>\n<p>Pilgrims came to her grave in the municipal cemetery. Taylor, who wrote a life of her in 1927, gave the figure of about 80,000 a year between 1898 and 1910. Some came barefoot, and a saying of the time compared praying there to praying at Lourdes. The Carmel kept a selection of the favors that people reported, and it filled seven volumes.</p>\n<p>Some of the reports reach back to the night she died. The community told of people pressing rosaries and medals against the body. A lay sister kissed her feet and was cured, it was said, of cerebral anemia, and a nun smelled violets. A child of ten smelled lilies, although only paper lilies stood in the room. The Carmel recorded these stories. The Church did not investigate them, and they belong to popular tradition.</p>\n<p>Some of the petals she had asked the sisters to keep entered the story too. In September 1910, at the hospital of the Little Sisters of the Poor in Lisieux, a man named Ferdinand Aubry lay with cancer of the tongue. Mother Agnes reports in a footnote that a rose petal from 1897 cured him. The Carmel reported this, but it was not one of the miracles the Church chose for her beatification.</p>\n<p>The 1912 book also carried words that her sisters recorded near the end, and Mother Agnes gave one of them in her closing chapter.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>“I have never given the Good God aught but love; it is with Love He will repay.”</p>\n<p><cite>— Thérèse of Lisieux, in Soeur Thérèse of Lisieux, 1912 edition</cite></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Her sisters outlived her by decades. Marie died in 1940, Léonie, who had become a Visitation nun at Caen, in 1944, and Mother Agnes in 1951, and Céline was still alive in 1952. Meanwhile the readers multiplied, and in 1907 Bishop Lemonnier of Bayeux and Lisieux asked the Carmelites to write down their memories of her and approved a prayer for her beatification. The question was no longer whether people loved her. It was whether the Church could prove that she was what they said.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"testing-the-witnesses\">Testing the Witnesses</h2>\n<p>The Church does not declare a saint because a book is popular. A cause must be opened, witnesses must be heard under oath, and the life must be examined for heroic virtue. In January 1909 the cause received a postulator, Father Rodrigue of St Francis of Paula, a Discalced Carmelite, and Monsignor de Teil was named vice-postulator in Paris. In the spring of 1910 the bishop ordered a search for her writings, and the court that examined them sat from February to June.</p>\n<p>The diocesan tribunal for her life was formed on 3 August 1910 and opened on 12 August. The historian Claude Langlois counts 93 sessions and 48 witnesses, who answered 30 questions. Three sisters were heard in August and September 1910, the others in February and March 1911, and only about half of them had known her.</p>\n<p>The witnesses did not agree with one another. Mother Agnes had taken notes of the last weeks and edited the book. Marie of the Sacred Heart, her godmother, was judged by Langlois the most sober and least calculated of the deponents. Céline, now Sister Geneviève of the Holy Face, gave testimony that he calls essential, too abundant, too sure, and close to Mother Agnes’s aims. The younger novices said that what mattered was how the young sisters had seen her every day, and one of them, Marie of the Trinity, came into open conflict with Geneviève.</p>\n<p>The process shows, too, how a testimony gets made. Monsignor de Teil drew up a list of articles in April 1910, and the sisters prepared their depositions with it in hand. Mother Agnes asked how she should cite Thérèse’s words, and Langlois uses that detail to show how the testimony was built.</p>\n<p>The body was examined as well. On 6 September 1910 the tribunal opened the grave for the first time, and only bones remained. The palm branch that had been placed in her hand in 1897 was fresh and whole. The remains were moved to a cemented vault in the middle of the Carmelite plot. A popular story says that on the night before, Thérèse spoke to Mother Carmela, the prioress at Gallipoli in Italy, and told her that they would find only her bones. Taylor reports it. It is a tradition with no deposition behind it.</p>\n<p>In 1911 a second process asked whether any public cult of her had arisen before the Church had ruled, and sixteen witnesses were heard. The diocesan process closed at Bayeux on 12 December 1911. In Rome the cause then passed to a critic. From January to April 1914 Monsignor Verde served as Promoter of the Faith, the official whose duty is to raise objections, and Anne and Claude Langlois have translated his objections for the first time.</p>\n<p>His doubts were sharp. He said she was admired mostly through her own book and that her humility was not heroic. Her deathbed words about relics and roses did not fit a humble person, he said, and Lisieux had run a publicity campaign. Such questions are the reason the office exists, and the cause would have to answer them.</p>\n<p>On 10 June 1914 Pope Pius X signed the decree that introduced the cause. Another source gives the day as 9 June, and the later narratives use the 10th. Then the war began and changed everything. The apostolic process opened on 17 March 1915 in the sacristy of Bayeux cathedral, and about twenty witnesses were heard, among them the priest who had heard her last confession. It closed on 30 October 1917.</p>\n<p>The judges did not examine her reputation for holiness in that process, because soldiers at the front had sent petitions for her beatification, and the petitions made the point plain. She was called the little sister of the trenches. The petitions from England and Canada stood out, and Benedict XV would later say, according to Monsignor de Teil, that English-speaking devotion showed the finger of God.</p>\n<p>The second exhumation took place on 9 and 10 August 1917, and two medical experts identified the bones. It was meant to be private, but thousands came. The bones were put into an oak chest inside a lead-lined rosewood chest. The record of the trial ran to about 2,500 pages, and about 2,000 of them concerned her virtue. Rome confirmed on 10 December 1918 that the documents were valid, and Benedict XV waived the usual wait of fifty years.</p>\n<p>A dark thread ran through the testimony. Langlois reports that in 1917 Mother Agnes wrote a document about the place where Thérèse had become a saint, and that it accused the former prioress, Marie de Gonzague. He says it fed a dark legend that ran against the official story. Marie de Gonzague had died before the trials began and could not answer, and the research for this account did not reach Mother Agnes’s own text or Gonzague’s side.</p>\n<p>On 14 August 1921 Benedict XV declared that Thérèse had lived the virtues to a heroic degree, and she became Venerable. In the same speech he spoke about her way of spiritual childhood. One question remained, and it was the one the crowds had been asking for years: what had she done since her death?</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"miracles-the-church-weighed\">Miracles the Church Weighed</h2>\n<p>A beatification needs miracles, and the Church asks doctors to judge them. Six cures were examined in the cause and two were chosen for beatification, each reviewed by three doctors. The other four are not named in the sources that the research could reach.</p>\n<p>The first belonged to a seminarian. In 1906 Charles Anne, twenty-three, from Lisieux, lay in the seminary of Bayeux with tuberculosis of the lung, with cavities and hemorrhage. A novena to Our Lady of Lourdes, made through Thérèse, had brought no cure, and so a second novena was made to Thérèse alone, to make her part clear. After a severe hemorrhage he cried out a sentence that the accounts preserve.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>“I did not come here to die! I came to work for God! You must cure me!”</p>\n<p><cite>— Charles Anne, seminarian, Bayeux, 1906, as reported by T. N. Taylor</cite></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>He fell asleep holding a relic that, in the account, had come mysteriously into his hands, and he woke cured. An X-ray taken fifteen years later showed that the cure had held, and the Church approved it as a miracle. One detail is odd. The superior of that seminary, Father Duboscq, later served as Promoter of the Faith in the cause, according to Taylor. The man who had been Anne’s superior was asked to raise objections to her case. Anne attended the beatification in 1923.</p>\n<p>The second case came from a convent at Ustarritz in the Basses-Pyrénées, where a sister of the Daughters of the Cross had suffered since 1913 from a stomach ulcer with hemorrhage. One source calls her Sister Aloysia of St Germain, while Taylor and the Latin decree call her Louise. In 1915 she received the last sacraments. In September 1916 a novena was made, and a scent came and went in the room. On the night of 10 September, she said, Thérèse appeared to her and promised that she would be cured. The two accounts give slightly different words.</p>\n<p>In the morning, rose petals of many colors lay on the floor by her bed. The sources disagree about the day of the cure, one giving 22 September and the other saying that she woke cured on 25 September. The Paris surgeon Dr. Le Bec reported on her, and X-rays were taken. A painting in St Peter’s shows the roses of this story.</p>\n<p>The Church approved both miracles on 11 February 1923, the feast of Lourdes. The general congregation had met on 30 January with the Pope present, and the final decree followed on 19 March. She could now be beatified.</p>\n<p>Two more cures were approved for her canonization. The first was in Parma. Sister Gabriel Trimusi, of the Sacred Hearts congregation, had had a swollen left knee since 1913, when she was twenty-three and broke firewood against it, and then the swelling spread to her spine. She was treated in a hospital with light therapy, and the doctors found tuberculosis of the knee and of the dorsal spine. She began a novena on 13 June 1923 before a small picture. On its last day, at a triduum at the Carmel, she walked there in great pain, and that evening she knelt without pain. Later she ran from her room crying that she was cured. The doctors said that neither chronic synovitis nor spondylitis had a natural cure. The Promoter argued that the cure counted as two miracles because there were two diseases, and the Congregation declined, accepting it as one, but a stronger one.</p>\n<p>Marie Pellemans, a Belgian, was the second, in March 1923. One source spells her first name Mary. She had tuberculosis of the lungs and intestines from October 1909, and she had been in a sanatorium at La Hulpe. In 1920 she went to Lourdes without result. She then made a pilgrimage to the Carmel of Lisieux, knelt at the tomb, asked for Thérèse’s help, and was cured at once. Three doctors examined her, and the Church approved the case.</p>\n<p>Not everything said about Thérèse passed through this process. Some biographies say that in 1922 the seven-year-old Édith Piaf regained her sight after a pilgrimage to the grave. The only source the research found is a secondary one, so the account cannot be stated as fact. A printed letter of 1923 tells of a man who had planned a murder and then his own death. He heard her words about spending Heaven doing good on earth, went into Notre-Dame des Victoires in Paris, saw them on the wall of the choir, and repented. The letter is anonymous, and it is a recorded testimony without any investigation.</p>\n<p>These accounts appear in full, with their status, in the miracles section of this page. They differ in weight. Some passed the Church’s examination, some are records kept by the Carmel, and some are popular tradition, and a reader can see the difference in each entry. The decree of 1923 meant that the promise made in the infirmary was about to be answered in public.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"roses-in-st-peters\">Roses in St Peter’s</h2>\n<p>On 25 March 1923 the remains were carried from the cemetery to the Carmel. More than two hundred priests took part, and about 50,000 pilgrims walked behind the rosewood casket. Taylor, who was there, reports that when the vault was opened a scent of roses filled the air. It is the report of one attendee, and it was not examined.</p>\n<p>The Pope who beatified her on 29 April 1923 was Pius XI, and she was the first person he beatified. He is reported to have called her the star of his pontificate. Two years later he would canonize her himself.</p>\n<p>The canonization took place on 17 May 1925 in St Peter’s Basilica, twenty-eight years after she had died in an infirmary bed. Taylor, who described it as a witness, counts 33 cardinals and about 200 prelates and a procession of about 4,000. Some 60,000 people were inside the basilica and about 500,000 in the square by evening. A wireless relay carried the Pope’s voice, which Taylor calls a first at a canonization, and at the end of the formula the crowd broke into applause, also a first, he says.</p>\n<p>During the Pope’s homily, Taylor reports, five roses fell from a cluster of lights in the apse and landed at his feet. It is an eyewitness report from someone who attended, and the Church makes no claim about it. That night, at the Pope’s wish, the old torches and oil lamps of the dome were lit again, and Taylor says the facade of the basilica looked like a giant rose of fire. The Pope called her, in Taylor’s record, a miracle of virtues and a prodigy of miracles.</p>\n<p>The next day French pilgrims met Pius XI, and he spoke to them of a deluge of glory on Lisieux and of his dear little Saint. On 30 September 1925, the anniversary of her death, Lisieux held its first feast after the canonization. The papal legate, Cardinal Vico, put a golden rose, blessed by the Pope, into the hand of the effigy, and the reliquary was carried through streets hung with roses. An aviator flew over the procession and showered it with roses.</p>\n<p>Here is the answer to the promise made in the infirmary. She had said she would spend her Heaven doing good on earth and that she would let fall a shower of roses. In 1925 the Church recognized her, half a million people stood in a square, and an aircraft dropped flowers over a street in Lisieux. The Church does not say that any of those flowers was a sign. It does say that she is a saint, and that the people who asked for her to be one were right.</p>\n<p>The objections of Monsignor Verde had been heard, and the Church had decided. Soldiers, doctors, and sick people had told their own stories by then, and the question of whether her humility was heroic had been asked and answered.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"after-the-roses\">After the Roses</h2>\n<p>In 1927 her feast was added to the General Roman Calendar for 3 October. On 14 December 1927 Pius XI named her patron of the missions, together with Francis Xavier, at the request of missionary bishops. The nun who had spent her religious life in a single convent in Lisieux had become their patron. In 1944 Pius XII named her co-patron of France with Joan of Arc, and since 1969 the feast has stood on 1 October. Reference works list other patronages too, among them florists and gardeners, aviators, the sick, orphans, and the homeless.</p>\n<p>The city of Lisieux built a basilica. Work began in 1929, and the crypt was finished in 1932. In 1944 bombing destroyed two-thirds of the city, and the basilica had only minor damage. The sources disagree on the date of its consecration, 1951 or 1954, and Pope John Paul II visited on 2 June 1980. The Carmel holds her remains, and the effigy of her, based on drawings and photographs, contains her ribs and other remains.</p>\n<p>In 1997, for the hundredth anniversary of her death, the Church took a further step. On 8 March the bishop of Bayeux and Lisieux, the Superior General of the Carmelites, and the postulator asked that she be named a Doctor of the Church. Consultors judged her doctrine eminent, and the cardinals and bishops voted unanimously in June. Pope John Paul II announced his decision at the Angelus during World Youth Day in Paris on 24 August. On 19 October, World Mission Sunday, he proclaimed her Doctor of the Universal Church in St Peter’s Square. His apostolic letter was called <em>Divini Amoris Scientia</em>, the science of divine love, and in it he wrote that the recognition of her holiness had been quick, universal, and constant.</p>\n<p>Her parents followed her. The Church beatified Louis and Zélie Martin at Lisieux on 19 October 2008. The cure that the Archbishop of Milan had accepted in 2004 was that of Pietro Schiliro, born in 2002, who had a lung disorder. A second miracle concerned Carmen Pérez Pons, a premature girl in Valencia with a brain hemorrhage, and on 18 October 2015 the couple were canonized together. Her sister Léonie has been named a Servant of God, and a diocesan inquiry opened on 2 July 2015.</p>\n<p>Her relics now travel. They began touring the world in 1994 and went to Ireland and Canada in 2001. In September and October 2009 they crossed England and Wales. On 1 October 2009 the casket spent a night in York Minster, an Anglican church, and about a quarter of a million people are reported to have venerated the relics during that tour. In South Africa they toured during the World Cup of 2010, and in 2008 the astronaut Ron Garan carried a relic aboard the space shuttle Discovery. The National Shrine of St Thérèse in Darien, Illinois, is said to hold the largest relic collection outside Lisieux.</p>\n<p>Whatever one makes of the stories, the promise itself was in her own voice. Near the end of her life she told the novices that if her little way led them wrong she would come back, and she said it as plainly as a teacher would.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>“Should I guide you wrongly by my little way of love, do not fear that I shall allow you to continue therein; I should soon come back to the earth”</p>\n<p><cite>— Thérèse of Lisieux, Counsels and Reminiscences</cite></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Her book is read in about fifty languages, and the Church, which once asked through Monsignor Verde whether she was heroic, now calls her a teacher. A woman who asked to be treated like a ball kicked into a corner has a basilica, a feast, and a pair of saints for parents.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"how-this-was-researched\">How This Life Was Researched</h2>\n<p>This account draws on three sets of research notes that list more than a dozen sources in English and French. They include her autobiography in the 1912 English and French editions, Mother Agnes’s notes of her last weeks, the archives of the Carmel of Lisieux, and Taylor’s and Giloteaux’s books from the 1920s. The Carmel’s transcripts of her three manuscripts were used to check the quotations and scenes from her own writing. Some quotations come from the edited 1912 text, as labeled. They also include John Paul II’s 1997 letter. The diocesan court in Lisieux held 93 sessions and heard 48 witnesses, according to Langlois. Some sources could not be reached, and the most important were the French critical edition of her last conversations and the full depositions. Where sources disagree, the text says so.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"sources\">Sources</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Agnes of Jesus, Mother. <em>Novissima Verba: Last Conversations and Confidences of St Thérèse, May–September 1897</em>. Neumann Press, 1952. <a href=\"https://archive.org/stream/NovissimaVerbaSt.ThereseOfLisieux/Novissima%20Verba%20-%20St.%20Therese%20of%20Lisieux_djvu.txt\">archive.org</a>.</li>\n<li>Agnes of Jesus, Mother. Deposition at the Ordinary Process, 1910, on Thérèse’s illness and death. <a href=\"https://carmelitequotes.blog/2026/09/29/tejpo30sep97/\">Carmelite Quotes</a>.</li>\n<li>Carmel of Lisieux Archives. “The Articles of Mgr de Teil.” <a href=\"https://archives.carmeldelisieux.fr/en/naissance-dune-sainte/les-proces-la-sainte-de-therese/les-articles/\">archives.carmeldelisieux.fr</a>.</li>\n<li>Carmel of Lisieux Archives. “The Making of the Story of a Soul.” <a href=\"https://archives.carmeldelisieux.fr/en/\">archives.carmeldelisieux.fr</a>.</li>\n<li>Carmel of Lisieux Archives. “Genesis of the Introduction of the Cause.” <a href=\"https://archives.carmeldelisieux.fr/en/\">archives.carmeldelisieux.fr</a>.</li>\n<li>Giloteaux, Paulin. <em>Saint Teresa of the Child Jesus: Her Supernatural Character</em>. 1928. <a href=\"https://archive.org/details/saintteresaofchi00gilo\">archive.org</a>.</li>\n<li>John Paul II. <em>Divini Amoris Scientia</em>. Apostolic Letter, 19 Oct. 1997. <a href=\"https://www.vatican.va/content/john-paul-ii/en/apost_letters/1997/documents/hf_jp-ii_apl_19101997_divini-amoris.html\">vatican.va</a>.</li>\n<li>Langlois, Claude. “Ordinary Trial Witnesses.” Carmel of Lisieux Archives. <a href=\"https://archives.carmeldelisieux.fr/en/\">archives.carmeldelisieux.fr</a>.</li>\n<li>Langlois, Claude. “The Apostolic Process” and “Testimonies of the Apostolic Process.” Carmel of Lisieux Archives. <a href=\"https://archives.carmeldelisieux.fr/en/\">archives.carmeldelisieux.fr</a>.</li>\n<li>Langlois, Anne, and Claude Langlois. “The Devil’s Advocate.” Carmel of Lisieux Archives. <a href=\"https://archives.carmeldelisieux.fr/en/\">archives.carmeldelisieux.fr</a>.</li>\n<li>Taylor, T. N. <em>Saint Thérèse of Lisieux, The Little Flower of Jesus</em>. 1927. <a href=\"https://archive.org/details/bwb_Y0-BYN-997\">archive.org</a>.</li>\n<li>Thérèse of Lisieux. Manuscripts A, B, and C. Transcripts. Carmel of Lisieux Archives. <a href=\"https://archives.carmeldelisieux.fr/en/oeuvres-de-therese/manuscrits/\">archives.carmeldelisieux.fr</a>.</li>\n<li>Thérèse of Lisieux. <em>Histoire d’une âme</em>. 1912 ed. <a href=\"https://fr.wikisource.org/wiki/Histoire_d’une_âme/1/1\">Wikisource</a>.</li>\n<li>Thérèse of Lisieux. <em>Soeur Thérèse of Lisieux</em>. Trans. Taylor. Burns Oates, 1912. <a href=\"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16772/pg16772.txt\">Project Gutenberg</a>.</li>\n<li>“Thérèse of Lisieux.” <em>Wikipedia</em>. <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Th%C3%A9r%C3%A8se_of_Lisieux\">en.wikipedia.org</a>. Used for checks only.</li>\n<li>“Basilica of Sainte-Thérèse, Lisieux.” <em>Wikipedia</em>. <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basilica_of_Sainte-Th%C3%A9r%C3%A8se,_Lisieux\">en.wikipedia.org</a>. Used for checks only.</li>\n</ul>",
