      {
        "time_period": "modern_era",
        "title": "Miracles attributed to Thérèse of Lisieux",
        "content": "<p>This list gathers the accounts attributed to Thérèse of Lisieux, with their sources and their status. Some were examined and approved by the Church. Some were recorded by the Carmel of Lisieux. Some are popular tradition. The list does not claim scientific proof for any of them, and it does not dismiss them.</p>\n<h2 id=\"approved\">Miracles approved by the Church</h2>\n<h3 id=\"charles-anne-1906\">Charles Anne, seminarian of Bayeux, 1906</h3>\n<p>Charles Anne, 23, from Lisieux, was a seminarian with cavitary tuberculosis and hemorrhage. A novena to Our Lady of Lourdes through Thérèse did not help. A second novena was made to Thérèse alone. After a severe hemorrhage he cried out that he had not come there to die, fell asleep holding a relic, and woke cured. An X-ray fifteen years later showed the cure had held. Three doctors examined the case. Status: approved for her beatification on 11 February 1923.</p>\n<p>Source: Giloteaux, Paulin, <em>Saint Teresa of the Child Jesus: Her Supernatural Character</em> (1928), https://archive.org/details/saintteresaofchi00gilo, pp. 199, 201-03; T. N. Taylor, <em>Saint Thérèse of Lisieux, The Little Flower of Jesus</em> (1927), https://archive.org/details/bwb_Y0-BYN-997</p>\n<h3 id=\"ustarritz-1916\">A sick nun at Ustarritz, September 1916</h3>\n<p>A sister of the Daughters of the Cross at Ustarritz (Basses-Pyrénées) had a stomach ulcer with hemorrhage from 1913 and received the last sacraments in 1915. After a novena she reported that Thérèse appeared on the night of 10 September and promised a cure. In the morning rose petals of many colors lay on the floor by her bed. She was cured, on 22 September in one source and 25 September in another. Dr. Le Bec of Paris reported, and X-rays were taken. One source names her Sister Aloysia of St Germain, and Taylor and the decree name her Louise. Status: approved for beatification on 11 February 1923.</p>\n<p>Source: Giloteaux, Paulin, <em>Saint Teresa of the Child Jesus: Her Supernatural Character</em> (1928), https://archive.org/details/saintteresaofchi00gilo; T. N. Taylor, <em>Saint Thérèse of Lisieux, The Little Flower of Jesus</em> (1927), https://archive.org/details/bwb_Y0-BYN-997</p>\n<h3 id=\"parma-1923\">Sister Gabriel Trimusi, Parma, 1923</h3>\n<p>Sister Gabriel Trimusi, of the Sacred Hearts congregation, had a swollen left knee from 1913, then pain in the spine. Doctors found tuberculosis of the knee and of the dorsal spine, and she was treated with light therapy. She began a novena on 13 June 1923 and on its last day walked with great pain to a triduum at the Carmel. That evening she knelt without pain, and later she ran from her room crying that she was cured. Doctors said there was no natural cure. Status: approved for her canonization. The Promoter argued it was two miracles, and the Congregation accepted it as one.</p>\n<p>Source: Giloteaux, Paulin, <em>Saint Teresa of the Child Jesus: Her Supernatural Character</em> (1928), https://archive.org/details/saintteresaofchi00gilo, pp. 213-17</p>\n<h3 id=\"pellemans-1923\">Marie Pellemans, Lisieux, March 1923</h3>\n<p>Marie Pellemans, a Belgian, had tuberculosis of the lungs and intestines from October 1909. She stayed in the sanatorium of La Hulpe and went to Lourdes in 1920 with no result. She went on pilgrimage to the Carmel, knelt at the tomb, invoked Thérèse, and was cured at once. Three doctors examined her. One source spells her first name Mary. Status: approved for canonization.</p>\n<p>Source: Giloteaux, Paulin, <em>Saint Teresa of the Child Jesus: Her Supernatural Character</em> (1928), https://archive.org/details/saintteresaofchi00gilo, p. 215</p>\n<h2 id=\"during-her-life\">During Thérèse’s life</h2>\n<h3 id=\"smile-of-mary-1883\">The smile of the statue, 1883</h3>\n<p>Thérèse, ten, was ill for weeks. She called for her sister Marie and at first could not recognize her. Her sisters knelt by the bed and prayed to the Virgin. She reported that the Virgin appeared to her and smiled, and she wrote that the healing that followed came gently, not all at once. Her father had paid for a novena of Masses at Notre-Dame des Victoires in Paris. Her own list gives 10 May 1883. The usual date is 13 May, Pentecost. Status: reported by Thérèse and her family. It is listed by John Paul II in 1997. It was not a miracle of the cause.</p>\n<p>Source: Thérèse of Lisieux, Manuscript A (Carmel of Lisieux Archives transcript), https://archives.carmeldelisieux.fr/en/archive/manuscrit-a/; Thérèse of Lisieux, <em>Soeur Thérèse of Lisieux</em>, trans. Taylor (Burns Oates, 1912), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16772/pg16772.txt; John Paul II, <em>Divini Amoris Scientia</em> (1997), https://www.vatican.va/content/john-paul-ii/en/apost_letters/1997/documents/hf_jp-ii_apl_19101997_divini-amoris.html</p>\n<h3 id=\"pranzini-1887\">A murderer and a crucifix, 1887</h3>\n<p>Thérèse prayed for the condemned murderer Pranzini, whom she called her first child, and asked for a sign of repentance. The day after his execution she read in La Croix that on the scaffold he kissed the crucifix three times. Status: her own report. The Church has not treated it as a miracle.</p>\n<p>Source: Thérèse of Lisieux, <em>Soeur Thérèse of Lisieux</em>, trans. Taylor (1912), ch. V, https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16772/pg16772.txt</p>\n<h2 id=\"after-her-death\">After Thérèse’s death</h2>\n<h3 id=\"night-of-death-1897\">Scents and a cure on the night of her death, 1897</h3>\n<p>As the community told it, a lay sister who kissed Thérèse’s feet was cured of cerebral anemia. One nun smelled violets. A child of ten smelled lilies, although only paper lilies stood in the room. Status: recorded by the Carmel and not investigated.</p>\n<p>Source: T. N. Taylor, <em>Saint Thérèse of Lisieux, The Little Flower of Jesus</em> (1927), https://archive.org/details/bwb_Y0-BYN-997, pp. 240-41</p>\n<h3 id=\"birds-and-sky-1897\">Birdsong and a clearing sky, 30 September 1897</h3>\n<p>Mother Agnes reports that birds sang in the tree outside through the whole agony and that the sky cleared at dusk. The sisters confirm the birds. Status: popular. The Church makes no miracle claim.</p>\n<p>Source: Agnes of Jesus, <em>Novissima Verba</em> (Neumann Press, 1952), https://archive.org/stream/NovissimaVerbaSt.ThereseOfLisieux/Novissima%20Verba%20-%20St.%20Therese%20of%20Lisieux_djvu.txt</p>\n<h3 id=\"palm-branch-1910\">A palm branch still green, 6 September 1910</h3>\n<p>At the first exhumation only bones remained, but the palm branch placed in her hand in 1897 was fresh and whole. Status: reported in the proceedings and by Taylor.</p>\n<p>Source: T. N. Taylor, <em>Saint Thérèse of Lisieux, The Little Flower of Jesus</em> (1927), https://archive.org/details/bwb_Y0-BYN-997, pp. 241, 245</p>\n<h3 id=\"ferdinand-aubry-1910\">Ferdinand Aubry and a rose petal, September 1910</h3>\n<p>Ferdinand Aubry had cancer of the tongue at the hospital of the Little Sisters of the Poor in Lisieux. Mother Agnes reports in a footnote that a rose petal from 1897 cured him. Status: reported by the Carmel. It was not a miracle of the cause.</p>\n<p>Source: Agnes of Jesus, <em>Novissima Verba</em> (1952), https://archive.org/stream/NovissimaVerbaSt.ThereseOfLisieux/Novissima%20Verba%20-%20St.%20Therese%20of%20Lisieux_djvu.txt</p>\n<h3 id=\"scent-of-roses-1923\">A scent of roses at the vault, 25 March 1923</h3>\n<p>When the vault was opened before the move of her relics to the Carmel, Taylor reports that a scent of roses filled the air. Status: popular report.</p>\n<p>Source: T. N. Taylor, <em>Saint Thérèse of Lisieux, The Little Flower of Jesus</em> (1927), https://archive.org/details/bwb_Y0-BYN-997, p. 252</p>\n<h3 id=\"five-roses-1925\">Five roses at the Pope’s feet, 17 May 1925</h3>\n<p>Taylor reports that during the canonization homily five roses fell from a cluster of lights in the apse and landed at the feet of Pius XI. Status: eyewitness report, not examined.</p>\n<p>Source: T. N. Taylor, <em>Saint Thérèse of Lisieux, The Little Flower of Jesus</em> (1927), https://archive.org/details/bwb_Y0-BYN-997, p. 10</p>\n<h3 id=\"man-at-notre-dame-des-victoires\">A man who planned a murder, 1923</h3>\n<p>A printed letter tells of a man who planned a murder and suicide, heard her words about spending Heaven doing good on earth, saw them on the choir wall of Notre-Dame des Victoires in Paris, and repented. Status: anonymous printed testimony.</p>\n<p>Source: Giloteaux, Paulin, <em>Saint Teresa of the Child Jesus: Her Supernatural Character</em> (1928), https://archive.org/details/saintteresaofchi00gilo, foreword</p>\n<h3 id=\"bishop-of-nardo\">The bishop of Nardò and Pastor Grant</h3>\n<p>A witness, the bishop of Nardò, reported hearing a word of Thérèse in an apparition, and the testimony was accepted at the trial. The Protestant Pastor Grant was also reported as converted through her. Status: testimony in the diocesan process, not an approved miracle.</p>\n<p>Source: Claude Langlois, “Ordinary Trial Witnesses,” Carmel of Lisieux Archives, https://archives.carmeldelisieux.fr/en/</p>\n<h3 id=\"ex-votos\">Ex-votos and favors</h3>\n<p>Fifty ex-votos were identified in 1911 and 460 by 1917. The Carmel kept a selection of reported favors that fills seven volumes. Status: Carmel records.</p>\n<p>Source: Claude Langlois, “The Apostolic Process,” Carmel of Lisieux Archives, https://archives.carmeldelisieux.fr/en/; T. N. Taylor, <em>Saint Thérèse of Lisieux, The Little Flower of Jesus</em> (1927), https://archive.org/details/bwb_Y0-BYN-997, pp. 247-48</p>\n<h3 id=\"edith-piaf-1922\">Édith Piaf, 1922</h3>\n<p>Some biographies say that Édith Piaf, aged seven, regained sight after a pilgrimage to the grave. Status: popular legend, found only in a secondary source. It is listed for completeness and not stated as fact.</p>\n<p>Source: “Thérèse of Lisieux,” Wikipedia, https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Th%C3%A9r%C3%A8se_of_Lisieux</p>\n"
      }
    ],
    "teachings": [
      {
        "time_period": "modern_era",
        "title": "Teachings of Thérèse of Lisieux",
        "content": "<h2 id=\"the-little-way\">The little way</h2>\n<p>Thérèse taught that one need not climb to God by great deeds, and that God lifts the small. She wrote it in Manuscript C in 1897 with an image of a lift.</p>\n<blockquote><p>“We live in an age of inventions; nowadays the rich need not trouble to climb the stairs, they have lifts instead.”</p><p><cite>— Story of a Soul, Manuscript C (1912 English translation)</cite></p></blockquote>\n<p>She wrote it for Mother Marie de Gonzague. She lived it in the laundry, where she tried to want more of the dirty water that a sister splashed on her.</p>\n<h2 id=\"love-as-vocation\">Love as a vocation</h2>\n<p>Looking for her place in the Church, she named love.</p>\n<blockquote><p>“O Jesus, my Love, at last I have found my vocation. My vocation is love!”</p><p><cite>— Story of a Soul, Manuscript B, September 1896 (1912 English translation)</cite></p></blockquote>\n<p>She offered herself to Merciful Love on 9 June 1895. She died saying that she loved God.</p>\n<h2 id=\"small-acts\">Small acts done for love</h2>\n<p>She told a novice that a small act done against laziness counts for more than a great one done in fervor.</p>\n<blockquote><p>“If you feel too lazy to pick up a bit of thread, and yet do so for love of Jesus, you acquire more merit than for a much nobler action done in a moment of fervour.”</p><p><cite>— Counsels and Reminiscences, recorded by a novice</cite></p></blockquote>\n<p>She led the ill Sister St. Peter to the refectory each evening until the old nun smiled.</p>\n<h2 id=\"trust\">Trust and staying little</h2>\n<blockquote><p>“We can never have too much confidence in the Good God, He is so mighty, so merciful.”</p><p><cite>— Counsels and Reminiscences, recorded by a novice</cite></p></blockquote>\n<p>She told a novice this. She lived it through the fog of doubt that began at Easter 1896 and lasted to her death, and she asked that the darkness be told so that other souls would not be discouraged.</p>\n<h2 id=\"after-death\">Doing good after death</h2>\n<blockquote><p>“Je veux passer mon Ciel à faire du bien sur la terre.”</p><p><cite>— Thérèse, 17 July 1897, as recorded by Mother Agnes (in English: I want to spend my Heaven doing good on earth)</cite></p></blockquote>\n<p>She said it in the infirmary at Lisieux. The Church canonized her in 1925 and named her co-patron of the missions in 1927.</p>\n"
      }
    ],
